<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Android Auto Backup rules.

    These previously read:

        include domain="sharedpref" path="."
        include domain="file" path="."

    which meant every case, every piece of evidence and the whole database were
    copied to the user's Google account backup. The comment explaining it argued
    only for preserving data across a device change; it did not weigh what
    sending privileged legal material to a third-party backup means. Google
    encrypts that backup, but under key material outside the app's and the law
    firm's control. That is a materially different trust boundary from "on the
    device", and not one to opt into silently on a client's behalf.

    Nothing is backed up now. android:allowBackup="false" in the manifest is the
    actual control; this file is kept, and kept empty of includes, so that
    adding one has to be a deliberate edit rather than an inherited default.

    Moving a case load between devices is served by the app's own cloud sync,
    which the user opts into and which targets storage they control.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <exclude domain="file" path="." />
    <exclude domain="database" path="." />
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
    <exclude domain="external" path="." />
</full-backup-content>
